---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { connectors, doc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['adapter', '1. Install the adapter'],
  ['connect', '2. Connect a service'],
  ['verify', '3. Test and use it'],
  ['catalog', 'Connector requirements'],
  ['custom', 'Custom MCP servers'],
  ['permissions', 'Permissions and troubleshooting'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Connect your services"
  description="Give the agent access to the tools you choose. Install the MCP adapter, authorize a service, then verify the connection before using it in a task."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="adapter">
    <h2>1. Install the MCP adapter</h2>
    <p>
      MCP is the protocol; a connector is a configured MCP server. pi does not include MCP in its
      core. Install <strong>MCP adapter</strong> from Settings → Extensions, or use:
    </p>
    <pre
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Install the MCP adapter"><code>pi install npm:pi-mcp-adapter</code></pre>
    <p>
      <strong>Settings → Extensions → MCP Connectors</strong> is visible even before installation. Its
      Advanced section also exposes adapter installation. A package declaration alone is not a successful
      service connection.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="connect">
    <h2>2. Add and authorize a service</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>MCP Connectors</strong>.</li><li>
        Find the service under <strong>Add a connector</strong>. Supply any required fields, such as
        the Datadog site or Slack client ID.
      </li><li>
        Choose <strong>Add</strong>. Phosphor writes the configuration and starts the adapter’s
        authorization flow.
      </li><li>
        Finish authorization in the browser. Review the requested scopes and the account or
        workspace you are granting access to.
      </li><li>
        Return to Phosphor. If the adapter explicitly asks for a callback URL, follow that prompt.
        Do not publish the URL or its authorization code.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-connectors')}
      alt="MCP Connectors showing connected servers, Test controls, and a catalog of services in an example configuration."
      caption="A connection has a status, a scope, and an explicit Test action"
      provenance="Settings demo · example data"
    />
    <p>
      The full-size capture also contains only example configuration. OAuth credentials belong to
      the adapter and its credential store, not to Phosphor.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="verify">
    <h2>3. Test the connection, then the task</h2>
    <p>
      Choose <strong>Test</strong> on the connected-server row. It opens a fresh connection through the
      adapter and can report Up with a tool count, Needs sign-in, Down, Disabled, Not in config, or Test
      inconclusive.
    </p>
    <p>
      Authorization and Test do not run a model, so they spend no model tokens. <strong
        >State unknown</strong
      > without a live session is not proof of failure. Cached tool names are not proof that a service
      is reachable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start a new session after changing configuration. Ask for a narrow read-only action, such as
      “Find the Linear issue I specify and summarize its acceptance criteria. Do not change it.”
      Inspect the tool result and source, not just the answer. Model-driven tool use consumes normal
      model usage and may use service quota.
    </p>
    <p>
      By default, the gateway lets the agent discover tool details on demand rather than sending
      every schema with every request. Direct-tool configuration bypasses that behavior and
      increases context use.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="catalog">
    <h2>Choose the right access for each service</h2>
    <div class="connector-requirements">
      {
        connectors.map(({ id, name, use, note, docs }) => (
          <article id={id}>
            <>
              <p class="eyebrow">{use}</p>
              <h3>{name}</h3>
              <p>{note}</p>
              <a href={docs}>{name} setup documentation ↗</a>
            </>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p>
      Available tools depend on the server, your account, and the scopes you authorize. Read-only
      defaults for Supabase and Questrade do not make every connector read-only. Supabase’s
      read-only endpoint does not narrow the OAuth grant: the token still carries write scopes.
      Restrict the endpoint to a project and review its permissions separately.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="custom">
    <h2>Add a custom MCP server</h2>
    <p>
      Use the custom-server control in MCP Connectors to provide either a server URL or a local
      command and arguments. Choose the intended scope. A command-based server executes a local
      program, so review it as carefully as an installed package.
    </p>
    <p>
      Test the server after saving. Start a new session to load it. Use Advanced for the raw
      configuration and resolution chain when a project entry overrides a global server with the
      same name.
    </p>
    <p>
      For Claude subscription sessions, configure servers here. Phosphor deliberately isolates the
      Claude CLI’s own MCP configuration and claude.ai connectors; they are not automatically
      imported.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="permissions">
    <h2>Permissions and common failures</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Needs sign-in:</strong> use Sign in or Reconnect for the intended account.</li><li
      >
        <strong>Down or inconclusive:</strong> check the server URL, network access, installed adapter,
        and local-command requirements; then Test again.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Slack Add is disabled:</strong> complete its registered-app setup, including client ID,
        redirect URI, PKCE, and scopes. The card’s “Set up the app” disclosure provides the manifest.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Tools are missing in a running conversation:</strong> start a new session after adding
        the server; the existing adapter read its config at startup.
      </li><li>
        <strong>The wrong configuration wins:</strong> inspect scope and shadowing in Advanced rather
        than duplicating credentials across files.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Authorize only the access needed. Some tools can modify external data. A worktree does not
      restrict a connected service, and an artifact’s sandbox does not restrict tool calls. Do not
      include credentials or private callback URLs in bug reports.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('mcp.md')}>MCP configuration, scope resolution, and OAuth reference</a> · <a
        href="/guides/extensions/#mcp">MCP adapter package</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
